---
title: Creating a Payment Gateway
description: Integrate payment providers into FOSSBilling.
---

This guide walks you through building a payment gateway adapter to integrate providers like Stripe, PayPal, or any payment processor into FOSSBilling.

## How Payment Gateways Work

1. **Payment Initiation** — Client clicks "Pay", FOSSBilling calls `getHtml()` to display the payment form
2. **Payment Processing** — Gateway processes the payment
3. **Verification** — Gateway calls `ipn.php`, which triggers `processTransaction()` to verify and record the payment

## Getting Started

### File Location

Place your adapter in:

{% filetree %}
- library/Payment/Adapter/
  - MyGateway.php              # Single file
  - MyGateway/
    - MyGateway.php          # Or subdirectory for complex gateways
{% /filetree %}

### Naming

- **File:** `MyGateway.php` (PascalCase)
- **Class:** `Payment_Adapter_MyGateway`

## Stripe One-Time Payment Walkthrough

The bundled Stripe adapter handles one-time invoice payments. [Stripe adapter source](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/0.8.9/src/library/Payment/Adapter/Stripe.php)

1. **Create a PaymentIntent.** The adapter uses the invoice amount and currency and stores the invoice, client, and gateway IDs as metadata. An idempotency key lets Stripe reuse the intent when a create request is retried with the same parameters. [Checkout code](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/0.8.9/src/library/Payment/Adapter/Stripe.php#L1750-L1770)
2. **Verify the callback.** The webhook handler verifies `Stripe-Signature` against the raw request body and checks that the event belongs to the gateway. The browser return handler fetches the intent from Stripe and checks the gateway ID and any linked invoice ID. In 0.8.9, only the browser return handler compares the payment currency with the invoice currency. [Webhook handler](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/0.8.9/src/library/Payment/Adapter/Stripe.php#L639-L706) · [Browser return handler](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/0.8.9/src/library/Payment/Adapter/Stripe.php#L346-L377)
3. **Check the status and amount.** Both paths require a `succeeded` PaymentIntent and convert its amount from minor units. For a linked invoice, `validatePaymentAmount()` compares the payment with the invoice total including tax. It rejects underpayments beyond a rounding tolerance and logs significant overpayments. [Amount validation](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/0.8.9/src/modules/Invoice/Service.php#L1127-L1143)
4. **Prevent duplicate credits.** Both paths take the same database lock for the gateway and PaymentIntent ID. The adapter skips payments already processed or in progress and invoices already paid. `claimForProcessing()` atomically claims the transaction row before applying credit. [Payment lock](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/0.8.9/src/library/Payment/Adapter/Stripe.php#L1022-L1123) · [Transaction claim](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/0.8.9/src/modules/Invoice/ServiceTransaction.php#L475-L505)

## Configuration Options

Use `getConfig()` to declare supported payment types and the settings shown in the admin panel.

FOSSBilling injects these into `$this->config`:

| Key | Purpose |
|-----|---------|
| `notify_url` | IPN/webhook callback URL |
| `return_url` | Browser return through `/invoice/payment-return` with status `ok` |
| `cancel_url` | Browser return through `/invoice/payment-return` with status `cancel` |
| `thankyou_url` | Browser return through `/invoice/payment-return` with status `thankyou` |
| `test_mode` | Whether in sandbox mode |

## Browser Returns

Use the injected `return_url`, `cancel_url`, and `thankyou_url` unchanged. These route through `/invoice/payment-return`, which navigates only to fixed invoice destinations using the supplied invoice hash and status. It does not accept arbitrary redirect URLs or restore a client session. The destination applies the normal invoice access checks.

Use top-level browser navigation for the return flow, as the bundled PayPal adapter does.

Keep `notify_url` for server-to-server notifications. A successful browser return is not proof of payment; verify the provider callback and payment details before applying funds or marking the invoice paid.

## Testing

Use this checklist to test your own adapter in test mode.

{% steps %}
1. Pay a test invoice. Confirm the record in **Invoicing → Transactions** and check that the invoice is paid.
2. If your adapter uses signed webhooks, verify that an invalid signature applies no credit. Test each crediting path for underpayments and payments in the wrong currency. Both should be rejected.
3. Redeliver the same successful webhook and confirm there is only one credit for the payment. If your adapter also processes a browser callback, test both arrival orders and concurrent delivery.
4. Test browser returns for success and cancellation, including an expired browser session.
{% /steps %}

## Examples

See existing adapters in the [source code](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/tree/main/src/library/Payment/Adapter/) for real-world implementations like Stripe and PayPal.
